    statement """
        All three pre-ISO lanes were told to keep a running COMMITS.md in ".agent/scratch/pre-iso/", but that is one shared directory and the filename is not lane-scoped, so Lane 1's file silently overwrote Lane 2's. Lane-scoped names (lane-2-COMMITS.md) fix it; the lane rules should name the file per lane.
        """
    observed_at git:3f796cf218ad2416629d9c5f793b1bf483e91505
    author "claude"
    created_at 2026-09-07
}

record legeos.papercut.an-acceptance-check-whose-method-is-command-is/1 : papercut {
    title "An acceptance check whose method is command is satisfied by the…"
    state verified
    statement """
        An acceptance check whose method is command is satisfied by the command's exit status alone, so a check that runs nothing is indistinguishable from a check that passed. cargo test -p <crate> <filter> exits 0 when the filter matches no test ('running 0 tests ... ok. 0 passed; 0 failed; 373 filtered out'), and a sweep of this workspace at e6e1b1c3 found 12 of 104 distinct test-name filters on live records naming a test that exists nowhere in the tracked source — 11.5 percent of the command gates could not fail, two of them backing verified evidence records that record result pass. This is the same species as AKR-G022/G023 unmatched globs, one layer up: a check that is unfalsifiable by construction. akr cannot parse every command, but it could ask evidence with method command to record what the command reported, or warn when a check's command has never been paired with evidence whose summary shows a non-zero count. Detecting it externally took eleven lines of Python over .akr/records plus git grep, which suggests a cheap built-in sweep for the cargo case at least.
        """
    observed_at git:e6e1b1c3523004f2eef22aa3bfd2dc2abae4a06a
    about "akr"
    author "opus"
    created_at 2026-09-08
}

