record legeos.papercut.lege-dev-deploy-failed-with-apt-unauthenticated/1 : papercut {
    title "`lege dev deploy` failed with apt \"unauthenticated packages\" right…"
    state verified
    statement """
        `lege dev deploy` failed with apt "unauthenticated packages" right after a fresh `lege dev repo init --install-source` on a machine with a stale cached index. Root cause: every publish regenerates the devrepo Release from scratch, so apt sometimes sees an Origin/Label mismatch vs. its cached copy and silently keeps the stale (mismatched) index instead of refreshing — unlike scripts/reinstall-legeos-desktop.sh, which uses dpkg -i directly and never touches apt's trust cache. Fixed by adding --allow-releaseinfo-change to the scoped apt-get update in refresh_index() (cli/lege/src/dev/install.rs); this repo has exactly one publisher so accepting the metadata change is always correct.
        """
    observed_at git:f5007ef369d0b386fb872a6b726e3f8baaac5944
    author "claude"
    created_at 2026-09-01
}

record legeos.papercut.lege-dev-deploy-restarts-lege-hostd-service-and/1 : papercut {
    title "`lege dev deploy` restarts lege-hostd.service and it crash-loops: zbus…"
    state verified
    statement """
        `lege dev deploy` restarts lege-hostd.service and it crash-loops: zbus fails "claiming org.legeos.Host on the system bus" with a raw EACCES connecting to /var/run/dbus/system_bus_socket (0666, root:root). Ruled out: AppArmor profile is confirmed complain-mode (would only log, not block); org.legeos.Host.conf is correctly shipped under /usr/share/dbus-1/system.d/; a plain `sudo -u root` shell can read/write the socket fine. Unit has no explicit sandboxing beyond ProtectHome=yes/NoNewPrivileges=no; RestrictAddressFamilies/SystemCallFilter show unset. Root cause still unknown — worth a fresh look with strace on the actual ExecStart process. Not blocking desktop-session testing (hostd isn't in the login path); packages were already fully installed by the time this surfaced.
        """
    observed_at git:f5007ef369d0b386fb872a6b726e3f8baaac5944
    author "claude"
    created_at 2026-09-01
}

record legeos.papercut.lege-dev-repo-verify-reports-a-blunt-verify/1 : papercut {
    title "`lege dev repo verify` reports a blunt \"verify: FAILED\" when the real…"
    state verified
    statement """
        `lege dev repo verify` reports a blunt "verify: FAILED" when the real problem is that /var/lib/legeos/devrepo/gnupg is drwx------ root:root and the command runs as the user, so gpg cannot read the keyring at all ("unsafe ownership on homedir", "Permission denied", then "Can't check signature: No public key"). The archive itself verified fine — both sets reported "ok (13 pinned package(s))" — so the only thing that failed was a check that cannot succeed for this user by construction. It should say it cannot read the archive's keyring as this user, rather than reporting the signature as unverified, which reads like tampering.
        """
    observed_at git:d9ab0344cdb3af1778b584c03b0ccf8707452bb8
    author "claude"
    created_at 2026-09-04
}

record legeos.papercut.lege-doctor-exited-1-on-every-developer-install/1 : papercut {
    title "`lege doctor` exited 1 on every developer install and made…"
    state verified
    statement """
        `lege doctor` exited 1 on every developer install and made scripts/reinstall-legeos-desktop.sh refuse a perfectly good session. Two checks asked image-time questions at runtime: os-release demanded ID=legeos on an Ubuntu build host, and mime-handlers resolved handlers against LegeOS app manifests only, so all 156 real .desktop entries in the user's own KDE mimeapps.list read as missing. The 4KB single-line detail then buried both fail rows, so the operator saw only "do not log out until its failures are fixed" with no visible failure. Fixed: os-release skips at root "/", mime-handlers resolves against every XDG applications dir (snap and flatpak exports always included, not just what XDG_DATA_DIRS happens to carry), and the CLI abbreviates detail and prints a closing tally naming the failures.
        """
    observed_at git:a185a0043a662c2923503984557948f598ee5220
    author "claude"
    created_at 2026-08-25
}

record legeos.papercut.live-text-console-boot-looks-hung-after-casper/1 : papercut {
    title "Live text-console boot looks hung after casper-md5check FAILED. Boot…"
    state verified
    statement """
        Live text-console boot looks hung after casper-md5check FAILED. Boot already reached multi-user.target; the ISO ships no md5sum.txt so the check always fails. Login is on serial-getty@ttyS0 because grub.cfg uses console=ttyS0/tty0, which does not spawn getty@tty1. Laptop then has no prompt. Mask md5check, pin getty@tty1, console=tty1.
        """
    observed_at git:e7458c3a132ed5ad8cd93f40d808bf2cef9456d4
    author "grok"
    created_at 2026-09-11
}

